            <ArticleTitle>Pressure-flow signatures of central-airway mucus plugging.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>223-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Medical Intensive Care Unit of Regions Hospital, a University of Minnesota-affiliated teaching hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT" NlmCategory="METHODS">Mechanically ventilated woman with status asthmaticus and acute respiratory failure.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Observations of airway pressure and flow tracings before and after bronchoscopic inspection and airway lavage.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Four newly observed signs were recorded that may serve to identify occult central airway mucus plugging in the ventilated asthmatic patient.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
